1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,325
The Rickshaw Men and
the Jinricksha Station

2
00:00:03,325 --> 00:00:07,671
At the corner of Neil Road and
Tanjong Pagar Road stands a small,

3
00:00:07,751 --> 00:00:12,165
ornate building of exposed brick
and white plaster, with an arched

4
00:00:12,245 --> 00:00:16,875
five-foot-way and a square corner
tower capped by an octagonal dome.

5
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:20,520
It is a wine bar, a restaurant
and some offices now,

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,325
and for a while the deed
carried Jackie Chan's name.

7
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:28,847
It was built in 1903 for a
single purpose: to register every

8
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:33,375
rickshaw in Singapore and
license the men who pulled them.

9
00:00:33,375 --> 00:00:36,915
At the peak there were close
to thirty thousand rickshaws

10
00:00:36,995 --> 00:00:40,536
on the streets and perhaps
fifty thousand men working the

11
00:00:40,616 --> 00:00:44,300
shafts in shifts, most of them
used up within a few years.

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:46,925
The building was preserved.

13
00:00:46,925 --> 00:00:49,725
The men left almost nothing.

14
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:53,330
Rickshaws reached Singapore
in 1880, the first batch

15
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:56,589
shipped from Shanghai
and the rest from Japan,

16
00:00:56,669 --> 00:01:00,425
where the vehicle had been
invented a decade earlier.

17
00:01:00,425 --> 00:01:03,359
They were cheaper and
quicker than the horse-drawn

18
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:05,710
gharries they soon put out of business,

19
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:08,925
and within a few years there
were thousands of them.

20
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:14,164
In 1888 the Municipal Commission
set up a Jinrikisha Department to

21
00:01:14,244 --> 00:01:19,725
register every rickshaw and inspect
it regularly for roadworthiness.

22
00:01:19,725 --> 00:01:23,890
The department worked out of
rented houses on Beach Road,

23
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:28,284
South Bridge Road and at Fort
Canning, then a purpose-built

24
00:01:28,364 --> 00:01:32,976
station at the Middle Road and
Prinsep Street junction in 1899,

25
00:01:33,056 --> 00:01:37,375
which cost 34,000 dollars and
was outgrown almost at once.

26
00:01:37,375 --> 00:01:42,064
In 1898 the Commission bought
a wedge of land where Neil Road

27
00:01:42,144 --> 00:01:47,225
meets Tanjong Pagar Road, from a
Johor noble, at a dollar a foot.

28
00:01:47,225 --> 00:01:51,667
The Jinrikisha Station was
designed by the municipal engineer

29
00:01:51,747 --> 00:01:56,412
Samuel Tomlinson and the municipal
architect David McLeod Craik,

30
00:01:56,492 --> 00:02:00,125
built through 1903 and
opened the following year.

31
00:02:00,125 --> 00:02:04,636
It followed the shape of its awkward
triangular site: a curved corner

32
00:02:04,716 --> 00:02:08,163
facade under a square tower
with an octagonal cupola,

33
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:14,085
exposed brickwork set off by white plaster
mouldings, and the Municipal Council's crest —

34
00:02:14,165 --> 00:02:17,825
a lion on an island beneath
a palm — over the entrance.

35
00:02:17,825 --> 00:02:21,870
Inside, clerks registered new
rickshaws, inspected the ones

36
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:26,075
already on the road, and issued
the pullers their licences.

37
00:02:26,075 --> 00:02:30,973
By 1902 there were 22,629
rickshaws to keep track of,

38
00:02:31,053 --> 00:02:36,125
and the number kept climbing
for another twenty years.

39
00:02:36,125 --> 00:02:39,581
The men who pulled were
overwhelmingly from two poor

40
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:42,777
coastal districts of
Fujian province — Hokchew,

41
00:02:42,857 --> 00:02:46,325
around Fuzhou, and Henghua,
further down the coast.

42
00:02:46,325 --> 00:02:50,200
Most were illiterate peasants
who had come south through the

43
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:54,300
coolie trade with a passage debt
to work off and little else.

44
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,895
Rickshaw-pulling asked for no skill,
only a back and a pair of legs,

45
00:02:58,975 --> 00:03:03,100
so it was often the first paid
work a new arrival could get.

46
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:06,775
Almost none of them owned
the rickshaw they pulled.

47
00:03:06,775 --> 00:03:10,861
A new one cost more than a puller
earned in a year, so the men

48
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:15,175
hired them by the shift from
owners who ran fleets of hundreds.

49
00:03:15,175 --> 00:03:18,036
Out of a day's takings
of roughly a dollar,

50
00:03:18,116 --> 00:03:22,425
twenty to thirty cents went
straight back to the owner as rent.

51
00:03:22,425 --> 00:03:25,829
What was left had to cover
food and a place to sleep,

52
00:03:25,909 --> 00:03:30,826
which for most meant a "coolie keng" —
a shophouse cubicle in Chinatown with

53
00:03:30,906 --> 00:03:35,494
plank bunks used in rotation by day
and night shifts, no running water,

54
00:03:35,574 --> 00:03:39,650
and, more often than not, an
opium lamp burning in the corner.

55
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:44,092
A working puller covered perhaps
twenty-five to forty kilometres

56
00:03:44,172 --> 00:03:48,473
a day at a jog, barefoot or in
straw sandals, through the heat

57
00:03:48,553 --> 00:03:53,075
and the traffic, and many chewed
or smoked opium to keep moving.

58
00:03:53,075 --> 00:03:56,510
The Straits Times in 1912
called rickshaw-pulling

59
00:03:56,590 --> 00:03:59,236
"the deadliest occupation in the East,

60
00:03:59,316 --> 00:04:02,975
and the most degrading for
human beings to pursue."

61
00:04:02,975 --> 00:04:06,866
James Francis Warren, whose
history of the trade was built

62
00:04:06,946 --> 00:04:10,427
from the colonial coroner's
records, found that most

63
00:04:10,507 --> 00:04:13,987
men lasted five to seven
years before a lung failed,

64
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:18,175
a tram or motor car caught them,
or they went home worn out.

65
00:04:18,175 --> 00:04:23,000
Sago Lane and its death houses
were a few streets from the stand.

66
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,950
The pullers were not silent
about their conditions.

67
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:31,082
The trade was shut down
by major strikes in 1919,

68
00:04:31,162 --> 00:04:35,723
1920 and 1938, usually
triggered by a new registration

69
00:04:35,803 --> 00:04:40,450
fee or rule, and the 1919
stoppage came as the price of

70
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:45,000
rice was climbing steeply
after the First World War.

71
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,238
Each time, the town fell
quiet for a few days,

72
00:04:48,318 --> 00:04:51,925
and each time the government
gave a little ground.

73
00:04:51,925 --> 00:04:55,227
The pedal trishaw was first licensed in

74
00:04:55,307 --> 00:04:58,775
Singapore in 1914, but it spread slowly.

75
00:04:58,775 --> 00:05:02,750
What really finished the
rickshaw was the motor car.

76
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:07,847
From the late 1920s the colonial
government began cutting the

77
00:05:07,927 --> 00:05:15,146
number of rickshaw licences in the interest
of road safety as motor traffic thickened,

78
00:05:15,226 --> 00:05:18,875
and by 1940 only 3,693
rickshaws were left.

79
00:05:18,875 --> 00:05:23,266
The Japanese Occupation handed
the trishaw its opening —

80
00:05:23,346 --> 00:05:26,859
with no fuel, tyres or
spare parts to be had,

81
00:05:26,939 --> 00:05:31,650
pedal power won by default —
and by 1945 it had taken over.

82
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:35,717
In 1947 the returning
British administration banned

83
00:05:35,797 --> 00:05:41,000
human-pulled rickshaws outright,
as an affront to human dignity.

84
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,775
Most of the men still pulling
switched to a trishaw and carried on.

85
00:05:45,775 --> 00:05:50,198
With no rickshaws to register,
the station was put to other work:

86
00:05:50,278 --> 00:05:54,850
a family-planning clinic, then a
maternal and child health centre.

87
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:59,761
In 1987 the Urban Redevelopment
Authority drew up the Tanjong Pagar

88
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:04,678
conservation area and offered the
building for tender on condition

89
00:06:04,758 --> 00:06:09,675
that whoever won it restored the
important features within a year.

90
00:06:09,675 --> 00:06:13,094
L&B Holdings took it in
1989 for close to two

91
00:06:13,174 --> 00:06:16,515
million dollars and
turned it into a seafood

92
00:06:16,595 --> 00:06:20,250
restaurant with shops
and a nightclub upstairs.

93
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:23,888
In December 2007 the Hong
Kong actor Jackie Chan

94
00:06:23,968 --> 00:06:27,529
bought the building for
eleven million dollars;

95
00:06:27,609 --> 00:06:31,250
it trades today under
its address, 1 Neil Road.

96
00:06:31,250 --> 00:06:34,525
The building was judged worth keeping.

97
00:06:34,525 --> 00:06:38,952
The trade it existed to regulate
produced no monument of its own —

98
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:42,299
a handful of studio
portraits taken as souvenirs,

99
00:06:42,379 --> 00:06:47,899
some newspaper reports of strikes, and the
shelves of coroner's inquest files that

100
00:06:47,979 --> 00:06:52,350
Warren read a lifetime later to
work out who these men had been.

101
00:06:52,350 --> 00:06:55,750
What is conserved is the architecture.

102
00:06:55,750 --> 00:06:59,309
The station's heritage signage
explains the brickwork,

103
00:06:59,389 --> 00:07:03,219
the octagonal tower and the
Municipal crest over the door;

104
00:07:03,299 --> 00:07:07,275
it does not mention the men
who queued there for a licence.

105
00:07:07,275 --> 00:07:10,919
A few streets away, Sago Lane
— where many of them lodged,

106
00:07:10,999 --> 00:07:15,157
and where the dying were carried
to the death houses — is a temple

107
00:07:15,237 --> 00:07:19,025
and a parade of shops now, with
little to show what it was.

108
00:07:19,025 --> 00:07:22,580
The merchants and philanthropists
of the same decades,

109
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:26,350
the men who held the capital,
are remembered in statues,

110
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:30,200
street names and foundations
that still give money away.

111
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:34,160
The pullers survive mainly as
background: a bronze figure

112
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:39,406
in an "old Singapore" street tableau,
a display case in a heritage centre,

113
00:07:39,486 --> 00:07:42,250
the pages of a single historian's book.

114
00:07:42,250 --> 00:07:45,806
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Early Singapore ran on

115
00:07:45,886 --> 00:07:48,996
imported muscle — rickshaw
pullers, wharf coolies,

116
00:07:49,076 --> 00:07:52,185
the men who dug the docks
and loaded the godowns —

117
00:07:52,265 --> 00:07:55,885
who arrived owing money, worked
until their bodies failed,

118
00:07:55,965 --> 00:08:00,175
and mostly went home or died without
leaving a name in the record.

119
00:08:00,175 --> 00:08:04,354
What tends to survive them is
the building that processed them:

120
00:08:04,434 --> 00:08:08,625
the registry office, the coolie
depot, the inspection station.

121
00:08:08,625 --> 00:08:11,434
The infrastructure of control outlasts

122
00:08:11,514 --> 00:08:14,175
the people it was built to control.
